#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""btrfs-bulk: ~/Bulk, one folder for everything that can be downloaded again.

Installed by `btrfs-setup.sh snapshots setup` / `btrfs-setup.sh bulk apply`
(CST) as /usr/local/sbin/btrfs-bulk. Runs as root; every change inside
the home is made by a child process that has dropped to the user (so a
symlink in the home can never make root write somewhere else).

The rule: everything in ~/Bulk can be downloaded or rebuilt again, so it gets
no snapshots and no NAS backup. ~/Bulk is one nested btrfs subvolume (normal
copy-on-write: checksums and compression stay on) with a CACHEDIR.TAG at its
root. Tools keep using their usual paths: each of those becomes a symlink into
~/Bulk (host, toolboxes and IDEs all see the same link).

  btrfs-bulk plan   --user U    dry run: what apply would do, item by item
  btrfs-bulk apply  --user U    do it (idempotent); busy items wait for the
                                next boot (flag /etc/btrfs-setup/bulk-at-boot)
  btrfs-bulk status --user U    state of every item, +C of ~/Bulk/emulators,
                                items waiting for the boot, old data left
  btrfs-bulk boot               (btrfs-bulk-boot.service, before login) apply
                                for the user in /etc/btrfs-setup/bulk.conf;
                                gives up after two boots
  btrfs-bulk keep ID...         keep these items in /home, by choice: apply
  btrfs-bulk unkeep ID...       leaves them alone (/etc/btrfs-setup/bulk-keep)
  btrfs-bulk steam plan|apply [--app ID]... [--copy] --user U
                                Steam closed: move installed games (not tools,
                                unless named with --app) from the Flatpak's
                                main library into the ~/Bulk/steam library
                                (reflink copy, check, renames, then both
                                libraryfolders.vdf, backed up first). NOCOW
                                (+C) game data cannot be reflinked into the
                                copy-on-write library: only with --copy is it
                                copied byte by byte (idle priority) and
                                compared before the old copy goes. Journal
                                journal-steam-<appid>.json, recovered like the
                                others (also at boot). Exit 0 in place, 1 needs
                                a look, 2 usage, 5 Steam is running (or busy).

Each item is declarative: absent -> created; already right -> nothing;
data at the old path -> moved into ~/Bulk, then the old path becomes a
symlink; anything unexpected (a file where a folder belongs, a symlink to
somewhere else, data on both sides) -> nothing is touched, it is reported.
A move copies into TARGET.bulk-new (reflinks: no data written, except for the
NOCOW emulator disks, which need a real copy), checks it, then swaps names; a
journal in /var/lib/btrfs-bulk makes an interrupted move recoverable (the
next run, or btrfs-bulk-boot.service before login, finishes or undoes it;
hourly snapshots wait while a journal exists). If both the old tree and the
new copy were written to during the switch, or the old path was created
again meanwhile, nothing is deleted: the journal stays and it is reported.
The same checks run when an interrupted switch is repaired (the journal keeps
both time stamps); once they passed, the journal says "finishing" before the
old tree is deleted, and a repair only goes on deleting it. A pure cache
whose tool deletes the link itself (`npm cache clean --force`, `go clean
-modcache`) and then fills a new real folder: the stale copy in ~/Bulk is
deleted and the new folder moved in, instead of "data in both". A journal whose paths are all inside ~/Bulk (the in-place
NOCOW rewrite) has "bulk_only": true: hourly snapshots do not wait for it.
Exit status of apply/boot: 0 everything in place (kept items count as in
place), 1 something needs a look (unexpected or failed; busy ones may wait
too), 4 only something inside ~/Bulk needs a look (the in-place NOCOW rewrite: no
data of it in /home; busy ones may wait too), 3 only busy items wait for the boot, 2 usage error.
"""

